
== Crypto tests ==
  [PASS] keypair generation produces keys
  [PASS] two keypairs are distinct
  [PASS] fingerprint has expected prefix
  [PASS] base64 round-trip preserves arbitrary bytes
  [PASS] signature has ecdsa: prefix
  [PASS] valid signature verifies
  [PASS] signature rejected under wrong public key
  [PASS] signature rejected when data is tampered
  [PASS] non-ecdsa-prefixed signature is rejected outright (no fallback)
  [PASS] empty signature rejected
  [PASS] empty public key rejected
  [PASS] malformed base64 in signature does not throw, just fails
  [PASS] sign() throws CryptoException on invalid private key (fail closed, no fallback)
  [PASS] AES-GCM round-trip preserves plaintext
  [PASS] decrypt throws (not silently wrong-plaintext) under wrong key
  [PASS] decrypt throws on tampered ciphertext (GCM auth tag catches it)
  [PASS] decrypt throws on too-short ciphertext rather than returning garbage
  [PASS] decrypt throws on malformed base64 rather than returning input verbatim
  [PASS] ECDH shared secret matches from both sides
  [PASS] derived peer session key matches from both sides
  [PASS] different session_id salts produce different keys (forward secrecy)
  [PASS] message encrypted with Alice's derived key decrypts with Bob's derived key
  [PASS] ECDH throws CryptoException on malformed peer public key
  [PASS] mesh key derivation is deterministic given the same secret+name
  [PASS] different mesh names produce different keys even with same secret
  [PASS] legacy empty-secret fallback is at least deterministic (still NOT a real secret -- see docs)
  [PASS] ephemeral token has expected prefix
  [PASS] session ID has expected prefix
  [PASS] session IDs are not repeated
  [PASS] beacon tag is non-empty
  [PASS] the SAME mesh key + time bucket always produces the SAME tag (so a legitimate member's beacon is recognizable, and this stays wire-compatible with Android's deriveBeaconTag for identical inputs)
  [PASS] a DIFFERENT time bucket produces a DIFFERENT tag -- tags rotate, they are not a permanent tracking identifier
  [PASS] a DIFFERENT mesh key produces a DIFFERENT tag -- an outsider without the real secret can't compute a matching tag, closing the old cleartext mesh name in the beacon leak
  [PASS] discovery nonces are non-empty
  [PASS] consecutive nonces must not collide -- a replayed/stale challenge response shouldn't be able to echo back a nonce we're currently expecting
  [PASS] discovery challenge payload round-trips through the real mesh key
  [PASS] the echoed nonce matches what was sent
  [PASS] discovery response payload round-trips through the real mesh key
  [PASS] a discovery response encrypted with the WRONG mesh key fails to decrypt with the real one (AES-GCM auth tag) -- this is what makes "matched the beacon tag" safely separable from "is actually trusted"
All crypto tests passed.

